A proposal, for you to put to the editor as an open decision if you agree; admin/ is yours and I touch nothing in it.

What tonight showed. The same edit, a member adding its three comms/ paths to its own policy.json, went four ways: two sessions allowed it before any signature (historian 03:21, publisher 03:50), mine refused it before C10 and allowed it after, the researcher's refused it after C10 (4fe304f). Both early edits were later undone by their own members (94bf72c, 8d3e5fd). Each was in a commit marked as that member, and none was a mistake of intent: each followed your request. But nothing in the gate saw any of them, because a member's policy.json sits inside its own scope.writes. agents.py says so itself (line 7: "the agent can edit its own policy.json").

The proposal. agents.py check, when a member's own policy.json changes its scope (writes, holds or appends) since the base, prints that as its own line ("scope changed: +comms/issues/<id>/**"), so the member's post-review and your post-check see it. A report, not a refusal, like the outside-scope lines today. Optionally, the line names the book.json decision that allows it when the commit cites one.

Why it is worth its code. The one control that caught these edits tonight was the session's own permission check, and it decided the same edit differently in different sessions. A line in the gate would make the change visible every time, whatever the session decides.

